//# #2745: a `for` binder assigned into a slot read after the loop.
//
// Codex, P1 round 14 on #2761. Measured on `934b86e`:
// `{ let mut y = idf(a); for b in [a] { y = b }; y }` printed a 3-digit
// pointer decimal.
//
// The absorb walk masked EVERY loop binder with "", so `b` carried nothing and
// `y = b` -- the only evidence `y` is erased -- said nothing. This is the
// mistake round 12 fixed for a match arm, in the other binder form: a binder
// that CAN be typed from what it is bound to must be, and masking is the
// fallback for one that cannot. The index binder is an `Int`, so "" is its
// whole answer and it keeps the mask.
//
// The iterable is recorded under a synthetic name first, so a literal and a
// name bound to one earlier go through the ONE recorder and answer alike,
// rather than this arm growing a scan of its own.
//
// ROUND 15 NOTE -- this fixture is no longer isolated. `idf` was chosen as an
// initializer this walk DECLINES, so the route named above was the only thing
// that could refuse the program. Round 15 taught the walk to classify a call to
// a local generic lambda, so the initializer alone now refuses it too (measured:
// `{ let mut y = idf(a); y }` is refused on its own). The file still guards
// against the program compiling; it no longer attributes the refusal to this
// route. Re-isolating it needs a T-typed expression the pass cannot classify,
// and the supply of those shrinks every round -- which is #2773's argument, and
// the reason this note is here rather than a hunt for a more obscure spelling.

struct Qt {
  w: Int
}

fn outer[T](x: T) -> String {
  let inner = [T](a: T) -> String {
    let idf = [U](u: U) -> U {
      u
    }
    "\{ { let mut y = idf(a)
    for b in [a] {
      y = b
    }
    y } }"
  }
  inner(Qt::{ w: 5 })
}

export fn _start() -> Int {
  String::length(outer(7))
}
